# GENERATED by mvp/scripts/build-audit.mjs from examples/toys/ - edit the
# toy or the spec, then regenerate; tests/audit.test.ts compares.

::f1 [F1: consilience, one to four confirmed consequences]: A free ground with one to four confirmed consequences, each pinned 0.9 behind a 0.8 line: the ground rises with each, 0.593, 0.667, 0.724, 0.767. A confirmed consequence is evidence for its ground, by Bayes.

  # f1a: one 0.8 line whose consequent is pinned 0.9
  @a-f1a [A, the ground]: free, nothing authors it
  @c1-f1a [C1, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  $f1-f1a [A brings C1] 0.8 @c1-f1a | @a-f1a: the line

  # f1b: two such lines
  @a-f1b [A, the ground]: free, nothing authors it
  @c1-f1b [C1, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  @c2-f1b [C2, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  $f1-f1b [A brings C1] 0.8 @c1-f1b | @a-f1b: the first line
  $f2-f1b [A brings C2] 0.8 @c2-f1b | @a-f1b: the second line

  # f1c: three
  @a-f1c [A, the ground]: free, nothing authors it
  @c1-f1c [C1, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  @c2-f1c [C2, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  @c3-f1c [C3, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  $f1-f1c [A brings C1] 0.8 @c1-f1c | @a-f1c: the first line
  $f2-f1c [A brings C2] 0.8 @c2-f1c | @a-f1c: the second line
  $f3-f1c [A brings C3] 0.8 @c3-f1c | @a-f1c: the third line

  # f1d: four
  @a-f1d [A, the ground]: free, nothing authors it
  @c1-f1d [C1, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  @c2-f1d [C2, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  @c3-f1d [C3, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  @c4-f1d [C4, a consequence] 0.9: confirmed
  $f1-f1d [A brings C1] 0.8 @c1-f1d | @a-f1d: the first line
  $f2-f1d [A brings C2] 0.8 @c2-f1d | @a-f1d: the second line
  $f3-f1d [A brings C3] 0.8 @c3-f1d | @a-f1d: the third line
  $f4-f1d [A brings C4] 0.8 @c4-f1d | @a-f1d: the fourth line

::f2 [F2: refutation, one to three failed consequences]: The same ground with one to three consequences pinned 0.2, failed: the ground falls with each, 0.225, 0.134, 0.081.

  # f2a: one 0.8 line whose consequent is pinned 0.2
  @a-f2a [A, the ground]: free, nothing authors it
  @c1-f2a [C1, a consequence] 0.2: disconfirmed
  $f1-f2a [A brings C1] 0.8 @c1-f2a | @a-f2a: the line

  # f2b: two such lines
  @a-f2b [A, the ground]: free, nothing authors it
  @c1-f2b [C1, a consequence] 0.2: disconfirmed
  @c2-f2b [C2, a consequence] 0.2: disconfirmed
  $f1-f2b [A brings C1] 0.8 @c1-f2b | @a-f2b: the first line
  $f2-f2b [A brings C2] 0.8 @c2-f2b | @a-f2b: the second line

  # f2c: three
  @a-f2c [A, the ground]: free, nothing authors it
  @c1-f2c [C1, a consequence] 0.2: disconfirmed
  @c2-f2c [C2, a consequence] 0.2: disconfirmed
  @c3-f2c [C3, a consequence] 0.2: disconfirmed
  $f1-f2c [A brings C1] 0.8 @c1-f2c | @a-f2c: the first line
  $f2-f2c [A brings C2] 0.8 @c2-f2c | @a-f2c: the second line
  $f3-f2c [A brings C3] 0.8 @c3-f2c | @a-f2c: the third line

::f3 [F3: the cancellation pair]: One free A arguing C and not C at the same strength: C reads 0.500 in every member. At 0.3 and at 0.5 A stays 0.500 too; at 0.8 the two lines promise more than one between them, and A falls to 0.192, the solve doubting the premise two contradictory lines share.

  # f3a: 0.3 for C and 0.3 for not C from one free A
  @a-f3a [A, the premise]: free, nothing authors it
  @c-f3a [C, the conclusion]: free, nothing authors it
  $for-f3a [A brings C] 0.3 @c-f3a | @a-f3a: the support
  $against-f3a [A brings not C] 0.3 ~@c-f3a | @a-f3a: the opposition, equal in strength

  # f3b: the same at 0.5, the sum of one
  @a-f3b [A, the premise]: free, nothing authors it
  @c-f3b [C, the conclusion]: free, nothing authors it
  $for-f3b [A brings C] 0.5 @c-f3b | @a-f3b: the support
  $against-f3b [A brings not C] 0.5 ~@c-f3b | @a-f3b: the opposition, equal in strength

  # f3c: the same at 0.8, past the sum of one
  @a-f3c [A, the premise]: free, nothing authors it
  @c-f3c [C, the conclusion]: free, nothing authors it
  $for-f3c [A brings C] 0.8 @c-f3c | @a-f3c: the support
  $against-f3c [A brings not C] 0.8 ~@c-f3c | @a-f3c: the opposition, equal in strength; the two rows cannot both hold

::f4 [F4: a chain read at both ends]: A implies B implies C at 0.8, all three free: 0.500, 0.700, 0.780. Each line is a leaky noisy-OR, one minus half of one minus the line applied to its premise, and having consequences pulls A nowhere below even odds.

  # f4: A implies B implies C at 0.8, all three free
  @a-f4 [A, the head]: free, nothing authors it
  @b-f4 [B, the middle]: derived, and itself a premise
  @c-f4 [C, the tail]: derived
  $ab-f4 [A implies B] 0.8 @b-f4 | @a-f4: the first link
  $bc-f4 [B implies C] 0.8 @c-f4 | @b-f4: the second link

::f5 [F5: when the tension tint lights]: The tension tint lights only where the solved value has left what the author wrote. A point 0.95 over a premise-less 0.2 floor reads 0.949, inside both: no tint. A 0.55/0.15 pair with one 0.8 support reads 0.794, inside its interval: no tint. A point 0.10 under the same floor reads 0.116, off the point and under the floor: the tint. The floor alone reads 0.500.

  # f5a: a point 0.95 above a premise-less 0.2 floor
  @c-f5a [C, the claim] 0.95: the author's point, above the floor
  $floor-f5a [at least a fifth] 0.2 @c-f5a: a premise-less floor on the same claim

  # f5b: a 0.55/0.15 pair with one 0.8 support from a 0.9 premise
  @p-f5b [P, the premise] 0.9: the support's premise
  @c-f5b [C, the claim] 0.55/0.15: the pair; the interval is 0.55 to 0.85
  $sup-f5b [P brings C] 0.8 @c-f5b | @p-f5b: one support into the paired claim

  # f5c: a point 0.10 below the 0.2 floor
  @c-f5c [C, the claim] 0.1: the author's point, below the floor
  $floor-f5c [at least a fifth] 0.2 @c-f5c: a premise-less floor on the same claim

  # f5d: the control: the 0.2 floor with nothing else
  @c-f5d [C, the claim]: free, nothing else authors it
  $floor-f5d [at least a fifth] 0.2 @c-f5d: a premise-less floor on the claim

::f6 [F6: explaining away]: Two causes A and B, each bringing C at 0.9. With C and A observed, B reads 0.515: A already explains C, so B is explained away. With only C observed, both causes rise alike, to 0.573. The map takes the Bayesian side of a known dispute in the logic of conditionals.

  # f6a: two causes of one effect; the effect and cause A observed
  @a-f6a [A, a cause] 1: observed
  @b-f6a [B, another possible cause]: free, nothing authors it
  @c-f6a [C, the effect] 1: observed
  $ea-f6a [A brings C] 0.9 @c-f6a | @a-f6a: the first cause's line
  $eb-f6a [B brings C] 0.9 @c-f6a | @b-f6a: the second cause's line

  # f6b: the same; only the effect observed
  @a-f6b [A, a possible cause]: free, nothing authors it
  @b-f6b [B, another possible cause]: free, nothing authors it
  @c-f6b [C, the effect] 1: observed
  $ea-f6b [A brings C] 0.9 @c-f6b | @a-f6b: the first cause's line
  $eb-f6b [B brings C] 0.9 @c-f6b | @b-f6b: the second cause's line
